    contradicts the no-backend decision we just made; it's the piece that forces the architecture to grow.

  The thing worth naming: the strongest version of "notify me" is the one we specifically ruled out by staying local.
  I'd rather you choose that tradeoff than discover it when a reminder doesn't arrive.

  [1mMy recommendation: in-app only, with a catch-up sweep on load[0m — on open, compute everything that became due or changed
  since your last visit and show it as unread. It degrades honestly (nothing pretends to be real-time), it needs no
  permission, and it's the same event-log query the OS-notification path would use later, so upgrading is additive
  rather than a rewrite.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should a notification actually reach the user? → In-app + catch-up sweep (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I put a design in front of you. This is the actual content of the feature: in a single-user app
  every task is "yours," so "tasks they care about" needs a concrete definition, and "change" needs a list.

  Pick the triggers that should produce a notification — I'd rather start with a narrow set that's obviously useful than
  a configurable engine nobody tunes.

